# Get under cover for the night: cut a staircase into the ground and wait in it
# until morning (decision 2026-11-09 "Five next areas"; the simplest shelter the
# existing primitives allow). A hole is not a sealed room: the entrance stays
# open, so a mob can still follow and the reflexes fight it in a one-wide tunnel,
# which is the point. What is left is a sealed shelter and a `sheltered` fact.
#
# Why the rules are ordered this way: the night ends the skill first. Then, if
# stone is not within five blocks, `dig_down` cuts the stairs until stone is
# within reach (stone lies three to six blocks under flat ground), which puts
# the body below the surface. With stone near, the body waits in short steps so
# the night check runs again. A `dig_down` that fails (water, a drop, no ground)
# falls through to waiting where the body stands.
#
# The radius is five, not three, because `dig_down` stops as soon as the wanted
# block is known within five blocks — the radius of the `mine` search
# (`EXPLORE_NEAR_RADIUS`). A rule that dug at three while the primitive stops at
# five would start a `dig_down` every tick as soon as stone lay four or five
# blocks off (a hillside, or the wall of a staircase already dug): each call
# confirms at once, so the night never ends and the body is stuck in that rule
# (review finding 1). Both sides now use one threshold.
#
# What is left: on ground that is already stone at the surface the rule never
# fires and the body waits in the open all night. Cover needs the designed
# `sheltered` fact and the `shelter()` primitive (survival design §3.6), not
# `dig_down`; see `backlog.md`.
skill take_shelter()
  doc "Dig a staircase into the ground and wait in it until the night is over."
  rules:
    not is_night() -> done
    not near(stone, 5) -> dig_down(stone)
    true -> wait(40)
